\pard\sb320\sa160\cf2\b\fs30 Abstract\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\fs22 Diabetes mellitus is a chronic condition affecting millions worldwide, and early prediction can significantly improve patient outcomes through timely intervention. This project applies and compares five supervised machine learning classification algorithms \f1\emdash\f0  Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) \f1\emdash\f0  to predict diabetes onset using the Pima Indians Diabetes Dataset (768 patient records, 8 clinical features). After preprocessing steps including missing-value imputation and feature scaling, all models were trained on an 80/20 train-test split and evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. The Decision Tree classifier achieved the best overall performance with 75.97% accuracy and an F1-score of 67.83%, outperforming the ensemble Random Forest model on this dataset. An interactive dashboard was also built to allow real-time diabetes risk prediction from patient input values. Results and limitations are discussed, along with directions for future improvement.\par
Keywords: Diabetes Prediction, Machine Learning, Classification, Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM, KNN, Healthcare Analytics\par

\pard\sb320\sa160\cf2\b\fs30 1. Introduction\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\fs22 Diabetes is among the most prevalent chronic diseases globally, and early diagnosis is critical to preventing long-term complications such as cardiovascular disease, kidney failure, and nerve damage. Traditional diagnostic methods rely on clinical testing, which can be costly, slow, and inaccessible in under-resourced settings. Machine learning offers a data-driven alternative: by learning patterns from historical patient records, predictive models can flag high-risk individuals for further clinical evaluation.\par
This project explores the application of supervised classification algorithms to predict diabetes based on routinely collected clinical measurements \f1\emdash\f0  such as glucose level, BMI, blood pressure, and age \f1\emdash\f0  without requiring specialized diagnostic tests. Multiple models are trained and compared to identify which algorithm generalizes best on this dataset, and the results are presented through visual analysis, a comparison table, and an interactive prediction dashboard.\par

\pard\sb320\sa160\cf2\b\fs30 2. Problem Statement + Objectives\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\fs22 Problem Statement: Given a set of clinical measurements for a patient, predict whether the patient is likely to be diabetic (binary classification: 1 = Diabetic, 0 = Non-Diabetic).\par
Objectives:\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-260\li420\sa100 Perform exploratory data analysis (EDA) to understand feature distributions and relationships with the target variable.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Preprocess the dataset by handling missing/invalid values and scaling features appropriately.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Train and compare at least three machine learning classification models.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Evaluate models using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrix.\par
{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}Identify the best-performing model and build a dashboard for real-time prediction.\par

