\pard\sa240\qc\cf1\i\fs20 Figure 6: Training Time Comparison \f1\emdash\f0  KNN and Decision Tree train fastest (under 4 milliseconds); Random Forest is slowest (~0.31s) due to building 200 trees.\cf0\i0\fs22\par

\pard\sb320\sa160\cf3\b\fs25 6.3 Confusion Matrix \f1\emdash\f0  Best Model (Decision Tree)\par

\pard\sb120\sa60\qc\cf0\b0\fs22\par

\pard\sa240\qc\cf1\i\fs20 Figure 7: Confusion Matrix for the Decision Tree model on the test set (Non-Diabetic vs Diabetic).\cf0\i0\fs22\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1 The diagonal cells represent correctly classified patients, while off-diagonal cells represent misclassifications. False negatives (an actual diabetic patient predicted as non-diabetic) are the most clinically significant error type, since a missed diagnosis delays treatment. The Decision Tree model achieved a Recall of 72.22% on the diabetic class, meaning it correctly identified roughly 7 out of every 10 diabetic patients in the test set \f1\emdash\f0  the highest recall among all five models.\par

\pard\sb320\sa160\cf3\b\fs25 6.4 Discussion\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\fs22 Interestingly, the single Decision Tree slightly outperformed the Random Forest ensemble on this dataset. This can be attributed to the relatively small dataset size (768 rows) \f1\emdash\f0  ensemble methods typically show their advantage on larger, noisier datasets, while a well-tuned shallow tree can generalize competitively on smaller, cleaner ones. Logistic Regression, despite being the simplest model, achieved reasonable accuracy but the lowest recall (50%), indicating it missed roughly half of the actual diabetic cases \f1\emdash\f0  a meaningful limitation for a medical screening context.\par
Accuracy alone is not sufficient to judge model quality here due to the ~65/35 class imbalance in the dataset; F1-score and recall are more informative, and both favor the Decision Tree model as the best overall choice for this task.\par

\pard\sb320\sa160\cf2\b\fs30 7. Conclusion + Future Work\par

\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\fs22 This project demonstrated a complete machine learning pipeline for diabetes prediction \f1\emdash\f0  from data preprocessing and exploratory analysis through model training, comparison, and evaluation. Among the five models tested, the Decision Tree classifier achieved the best balance of accuracy (75.97%), recall (72.22%), and F1-score (67.83%), making it the recommended model for this dataset and use case.\par
Limitations: The dataset is relatively small (768 records) and drawn from a single demographic group (Pima Indian women), which may limit generalizability to broader populations. The moderate class imbalance also constrains how much further performance gains are achievable without resampling techniques.\par
Future Work:\par

\pard{\pntext\f2\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf2\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-260\li420\sa100 Apply class balancing techniques such as SMOTE or class-weighted loss functions to improve recall further.\par
